      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Low-back pain may originate in the thoraco-lumbar joints. Due to motion and stress, the latter constitute a high-risk transitional zone. Pain is transmitted by the posterior branches of the D11, D12 or L1 spinal nerves, which innervate the cutaneous and subcutaneous levels of the low-back and upper buttocks region. It is experienced as a deep-seated pain. Clinical examination makes it possible to determine the level responsible. The lumbar pain disappears with anesthesia of the interapophysary articulation. Appropriate medical treatment most often succeeds. If its fails, surgery can provide a solution. It consists of a capsulectomy on the level responsible, and on the upper and lower adjoining regions. This operation also destroys the posterior branch, which is closely joined to the capsule. Out of the 10 cases operated on, there was 1 failure, 6 very good results and 3 good results after follow-up periods of 20 months to 6 months. Without prejudging the future, it seems that there is hope for the treatment of unexplained back pains or those persisting after operations on lumbar disks that are not relieved by appropriate medical treatment of the thoraco-lumbar joints."},
